/* QL:         list */
/* QL: * The original algorithm was based on the function value at the mid-point */
/* QL: * of the line between the square or diagonal move's finish points. */
/* QL: * I have changed this to be the average of the function values at those two */
/* QL: * points, which ensures that the algorithm tracks its way along using points */
/* QL: * whose functional values are the closest to zero of those available. */
/* QL: * The original algorithm failed to do this, and as a result was liable to go */
/* QL: * astray when presented with nearly equal values, as the mid point value was */
/* QL: * not giving the correct idea of which point was nearest the curve. This would */
/* QL: * become somewhat unpredictable for small ellipses, whereas my method is at */
/* QL: * least predictable. lwr. */
/* QL: * The algorithm now goes like this: */
/* QL: * 1) If the start and end points are the same, lines and arcs of less than five */
/* QL: *       octants stop without drawing anything. */
/* QL: * 2) Draw the first point. */
/* QL: * 4) If the octant count is greater than four, draw the next point, unless it */
/* QL: *       repeats the start or actually is the finish. This helps with small arcs */
/* QL: *       and ellipses. */
/* QL: * 5) Thereafter, when we get the octant count down less than three, if we're */
/* QL: *       about to draw a point adjacent to the finish point, do so and stop in */
/* QL: *       all cases except degenerate ellipses of more than one pixel. */
/* QL: * This is still not perfect, as silly little arcs may overshoot by one pixel. */
/* QL: * Also, this now checks for octant changing at the right time, i.e. just when */
/* QL: * "d" is about to be updated. If the selected move takes "d" further away from */
/* QL: * zero, that's the time to do an octant change (if there are any left!). */
